# Singly Linked List

Implementation of a singly linked list in Python.

## Approach

The linked list is represented using `ListNode` objects, where each node stores:

- `val`: the value stored in the node
- `next`: a reference to the next node

The `LinkedList` class maintains a reference to the first node through `head`.

### Operations

| Operation | Complexity |
|---|---:|
| `get(i)` | O(n) |
| `insertHead(val)` | O(1) |
| `insertTail(val)` | O(n) |
| `remove(i)` | O(n) |
| `getValues()` | O(n) |

## Key Takeaway

A singly linked list provides constant-time insertion at the head because only the `head` reference needs to be updated.

Operations involving a specific index require traversing the list from the head, resulting in O(n) time complexity.